course: dictionary
lesson: 8683
title: Seem: looks can fool you
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: To seem means to look or appear to be something, going by what you can see or know. «She seems happy» means she looks happy to you, but you cannot be completely sure what she feels inside.
explain: Use «it seems» when you think something is true but you are not sure yet. «It seems we are lost» means you think you are lost, but you have not checked. Scientists use the same idea: a result «seems to show» something until it has been tested.
explain: Things that seem one way can turn out to be different. In a story, «There seemed to be no end to this wood» tells you what the character thinks, not what is certainly true. Always ask: is this how it seems, or how it really is?
fact: Seem means to look or appear to be something.
fact: «It seems» is used when you think something is true but are not sure.
fact: The forms are seem, seems, seemed and seeming.
fact: Useful phrases are «seem to», «it seems that» and «seem like».
fact: Someone who seems friendly online may not be, so check before you trust them.
exercise: «She seems happy.» What does seems mean here? | Is sad and tired | Is definitely happy, with proof | *Looks or appears to be happy | Is far away
exercise: Ben says «It seems we are lost.» What does he think? | He is lost, and it makes no difference | He knows for certain they are lost | *He thinks they are lost, but he is not sure | They have already been found
exercise: «The Sun seems to move because the Earth spins.» Is the Sun really moving across the sky? | Yes, it moves across the sky on its own | *No, it only looks as if it moves | Only in winter | Only at night
test: Choose the right form: «Yesterday the path ___ safe, but it was not.» | seeming | *seemed | seam | seems
test: «There seemed to be no end to this wood.» What does the author mean? | The wood had a gate at the far end | The wood was short and easy to cross | *The wood looked as if it went on forever
exam: A thinking trap can make things «seem worse, bigger or more certain than they are». What does seem mean here? | Fall down slowly | Disappear completely | *Look or appear to be, so things may not be as they really are | Be exactly the same as the truth
